3.text:
**Answer: Benin and Madagascar.**

I agree with Colleagues 2 and 4. The key is the discretization convention implied by “rank is maximal.” The Danish flag gives a \(3\times 3\) color matrix of rank \(2\): the rows are either \(r,w,r\) or \(w,w,w\). To match this, an African flag must have a purely rectangular color pattern whose maximal matrix rank is exactly \(2\).

- **Benin**:  
  \[
  \begin{pmatrix}
  \text{green} & \text{yellow}\\
  \text{green} & \text{red}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \]
  has rank \(2\) for distinct colors.

- **Madagascar**:  
  \[
  \begin{pmatrix}
  \text{white} & \text{red}\\
  \text{white} & \text{green}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \]
  also has rank \(2\).

Other suggested flags either are simple horizontal/vertical stripes (rank \(1\)) or contain non-rectangular features such as stars, triangles, crescents, or coats of arms. Under maximal-rank treatment, those non-rectangular features cannot be collapsed into a single cell without changing the rank; their varying boundaries create additional independent row/column patterns, raising the rank to at least \(3\). Thus Djibouti, Guinea-Bissau, Sudan, Senegal, Cameroon, Egypt, Ghana, Lesotho, Liberia, Malawi, Togo, etc., do not match Denmark’s rank \(2\) under this standard interpretation.

So the correct African flags with the same maximal linear algebraic rank as Denmark are **Benin and Madagascar**.

